{"surface": "inspector", "title": "Full text", "html": "<header class=\"hero\"><p class=\"kicker\">Full text</p><h2 class=\"title\">benzinga.com</h2><p class=\"ctx\"><a href=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/prediction-markets/26/10/62724648/el-sayed-ai-ownership\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">benzinga.com/markets/prediction-markets/26/10/62724648/el-sayed-ai-ownership</a> · read Nov 19, 2026</p></header><div class=\"page md\" data-md=\"# El-Sayed Leads Michigan Senate Poll by 7 Points, Wants 50% Public Ownership of OpenAI, Anthropic\n\nDemocrat **Abdul El-Sayed** wants Americans to own half of major AI developers, including Anthropic, which filed for an IPO Monday, and OpenAI. A new CNN poll shows him leading Michigan’s Senate race by seven points.\n\nThe [survey](https://edition.cnn.com/2026/10/29/politics/cnn-polls-michigan-maine-texas-kansas-senate-races-midterms-vis) released Thursday puts El-Sayed ahead of Republican **Mike Rogers**, 49% to 42%, among 768 likely voters, with a 4.7-point margin of error.\n\nOn [Polymarket](https://polymarket.com/event/michigan-senate-election-winner), traders put El-Sayed’s election chances near 72%, with about \\$3 million traded. His polling lead exceeds the 3.2-point [average](https://www.realclearpolling.com/polls/senate/general/2026/michigan/rogers-vs-el-sayed) tracked by RealClearPolling.\n\n### Americans Would Own Half of AI Giants\n\nEl-Sayed backs Sen. **Bernie Sanders’** (I-VT) [proposal](https://www.sanders.senate.gov/press-releases/news-sanders-introduces-legislation-to-create-7-trillion-ai-sovereign-wealth-fund/) to impose a one-time 50% stock tax on AI companies with more than \\$200 million in annual AI sales, paid in newly issued shares.\n\nThe shares would enter a public sovereign wealth fund that Sanders estimates could be worth \\$7 trillion. He says an annual payout equal to 5% of the fund’s value could provide every American with more than \\$1,000.\n\n“I want to actually take it a step further,” El-Sayed [said](https://politifact.com/article/2026/aug/19/do-mi-senate-candidate-el-sayeds-comments-about-public-ownership-show-he-supports-communism/) in June when asked about Sanders’ public-ownership proposal.\n\nHis [platform](https://abdulforsenate.com/2026/06/first-do-no-harm-ai-under-democracy/) would give publicly appointed or elected directors a majority of board seats at frontier AI companies.\n\n**Microsoft Corp.** [](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/MSFT)[(NASDAQ:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/MSFT)[MSFT](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/MSFT)) owns about 24% of OpenAI. **Amazon.com Inc.** [](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMZN)[(NASDAQ:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMZN)[AMZN](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMZN)) owns an estimated 9% of Anthropic, while **Alphabet Inc.** [](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/GOOGL)[(NASDAQ:](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/GOOGL)[GOOGL](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/GOOGL)) holds at least 6.6%, according to [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-26/anthropic-ipo-filing-cites-reliance-on-google-amazon-microsoft).\n\nUnder Sanders’ proposal, Microsoft’s stake in OpenAI would fall to about 12%. Google and Amazon would see their percentage ownership in Anthropic halved too.\n\nEl-Sayed’s expanded October plan also seeks to pause frontier AI development and new data centers until safety standards are established. Rogers separately supports a one-year moratorium on new Michigan data centers.\n\nUniversity of Michigan professor **Elizabeth Anderson** told [PolitiFact](https://politifact.com/article/2026/aug/19/do-mi-senate-candidate-el-sayeds-comments-about-public-ownership-show-he-supports-communism/) the mandatory equity transfer “sounds like outright expropriation,” questioning its constitutionality. Sanders’ legislation has not passed Congress.\n\n### Anthropic Pledges 5%, El-Sayed Wants 50%\n\nAnthropic [announced](https://www.anthropic.com/news/public-benefit-fund) October 18 that it would dedicate an initial 5% of its shares to a wholly owned public-benefit fund, subject to shareholder approval.\n\nThe fund could finance charitable grants or direct payments to specific populations. Unlike El-Sayed’s proposal, Anthropic would retain control over the fund.\n\nAnthropic plans to list on Nasdaq under the ticker ANT. Its [formal investor roadshow](https://www.benzinga.com/watch/anthropic-ipo-claude-maker-files-s-1-nasdaq-listing-under-ant-62599364) could begin as early as November 10, one week after Michigan voters choose their next senator.\n\n*Image: Shutterstock*\n\n**Read Also: [Microsoft&#x27;s \\$28 Billion Cash Flow Edge Over Google Gives It an AI Advantage, Analyst Says](https://www.benzinga.com/markets/prediction-markets/26/10/62704431/microsoft-google-cash-flow)**\" data-base=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/prediction-markets/26/10/62724648/el-sayed-ai-ownership\"></div><p class=\"raw\"><a class=\"rawlink\" data-go=\"raw:src:192\">Full details</a></p>", "wide": true}